 spending remains minimal"], "rationale": "Markets and raters converge on Adam Gray as a clear favorite, and his fundraising advantage and incumbency in a slightly Democratic-leaning, newly redrawn district outweigh early Republican investment; with no public general-election polls, uncertainty stays elevated.", "watch": "A late surge of Republican outside spending or a weaker national environment for Democrats could pull the margin into the single digits.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Adam Gray (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Adam Gray, the Democratic incumbent, seeks another term in a Central Valley district redrawn to add Stockton and now leaning Democratic by about two points. He enters the general election with a clear financial edge, having raised roughly $5.1 million to Kevin Lincoln's $2.9 million, and carries incumbency advantages in a year when polls show declining Republican approval of Trump in California. Prediction markets give Gray about a 95 percent chance of winning, and most nonpartisan raters place the race in the Likely or Lean Democratic column, though a few call it closer.\n\nLincoln, a former mayor, advanced through the primary and has drawn growing Republican interest, with one outlet describing the district as a tossup and noting fresh GOP investment. Outside spending so far remains modest on both sides. With no public general-election polling available, the outlook rests on fundamentals, ratings, and market pricing, leaving some room for the race to tighten.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9502, "margin": 10.95}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9343, "margin": 10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.945, "margin": 11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.941, "margin": 10.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9142, "margin": 10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9277, "margin": 10.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem"